WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.275
Rust's ownership model gives each value a
responsible owner.

2
00:00:03.355 --> 00:00:06.718
The owner determines when the value is
available and when cleanup happens.

3
00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:10.000
When the owner goes out of scope, Rust
runs drop behavior for that value.

4
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.603
This gives ordinary safe code predictable
cleanup without manual free calls.

5
00:00:13.683 --> 00:00:17.099
When a non-Copy value is moved, ownership
transfers to a new binding, field,

6
00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:18.887
return value, or function parameter.

7
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:22.116
The original binding can no longer be used
as if it still owns the value.

8
00:00:22.196 --> 00:00:25.985
That rule prevents two parts of the
program from both believing they are responsible

9
00:00:26.065 --> 00:00:27.080
for the same resource.

10
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.508
Move semantics are a major use-after-free
defense.

11
00:00:29.588 --> 00:00:30.922
If a value has been handed off,

12
00:00:31.002 --> 00:00:33.404
Rust does not allow the old owner to
continue using it.

13
00:00:33.484 --> 00:00:35.406
Instead of letting stale access continue,

14
00:00:35.486 --> 00:00:38.154
the compiler forces the developer to
choose a clear design:

15
00:00:38.234 --> 00:00:41.170
borrow temporarily, transfer ownership,
clone intentionally,

16
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.477
or return ownership later.

17
00:00:42.557 --> 00:00:44.319
`Drop` connects ownership to cleanup.

18
00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:47.494
It can release memory, close files,
release sockets, unlock guards,

19
00:00:47.574 --> 00:00:51.524
decrement reference counts, or perform
other structured cleanup when ownership ends.

20
00:00:51.604 --> 00:00:55.554
Reviewers should understand drop behavior
for types that manage important resources,

21
00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:58.356
especially around error paths and partial
initialization.

22
00:00:58.436 --> 00:01:01.585
Defensive Rust programming treats moves
and drops as design signals.

23
00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:03.640
Ownership transfer should be intentional.

24
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.161
Cleanup should be predictable.

25
00:01:05.241 --> 00:01:08.016
APIs should make it clear whether a caller
is giving up a value,

26
00:01:08.096 --> 00:01:10.658
borrowing it temporarily, or receiving an
owned result.

27
00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:13.460
That clarity is what prevents stale
lifecycle assumptions.
